WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
you'll hear lots of people say&nbsp;
you have to use sulfate free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:05.160
shampoo to avoid damaging or stripping your hair

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:09.000
you'll also hear people say you have&nbsp;
to use sulfates to get your hair clean

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.740
they are both wrong

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:15.360
almost everyone misunderstands this&nbsp;
fundamental thing about shampoos and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.480
I think it's why so many people&nbsp;
can't find the right products

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:23.460
I'm Michelle of lab muffin beauty science&nbsp;
chemistry PhD and cosmetic chemist and person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:27.780
who usually talks more about skincare&nbsp;
science but we are doing hair today

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:32.100
I've been paying more attention to hair&nbsp;
science on the internet lately and there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.960
so many completely wrong explanations and strong&nbsp;
opinions and just people yelling at each other

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:43.380
but because I love pain apparently&nbsp;
I am going to talk about it

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:46.320
I'm starting with sulfate&nbsp;
versus sulfate-free shampoos

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:51.000
I've talked about it a bit before but I'm&nbsp;
going into more depth because I think if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.680
we really dig into the chemistry of it a lot&nbsp;
of things about hair start making more sense

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:00.000
but before we talk about sulfates we have&nbsp;
to talk about the state of hair science

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.960
not just internet hair science&nbsp;
but actual academic hair science

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.280
it is a mess

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.040
there are three big reasons I&nbsp;
think there's so much confusion

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:12.840
reason one hair is really&nbsp;
diverse so much more than skin

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.680
different hair just does not respond&nbsp;
to hair care products the same way

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:21.900
skin care seems complex but when you break&nbsp;
it down it's actually pretty straightforward

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.680
maybe also it seems that way to me because&nbsp;
I've been talking about it for so long

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.500
with skin type there's oily and dry and in between

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.800
then you have concerns like&nbsp;
fine lines pigmentation acne

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:35.880
it does take a while to understand your&nbsp;
skin and find the right products for it

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.340
I have to plug my ebook because&nbsp;
I keep forgetting to do that

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:39.960
it walks you through that process

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:44.280
but when it comes down to it ingredients&nbsp;
tend to work pretty similarly for everyone

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:48.840
if you have a hundred people use glycerin&nbsp;
on their skin you might have 60 people's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:52.980
skin get more hydrated and improve 20&nbsp;
people's skin stays around the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:57.000
and the final 20 might get worse for&nbsp;
some reason like the glycerin helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.320
some other ingredients penetrate and the&nbsp;
skin got irritated or they have an allergy

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:05.640
there is still complexity but you wouldn't&nbsp;
have like half the people's skin dry out more

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:08.040
but that's kind of how it is with hair

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:12.960
even just with the shape of the strands you&nbsp;
have straight curly a million different levels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:17.880
and types of curly kinky tight coils the&nbsp;
coils might be rounder or flatter or a mix

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:22.140
and shape makes a massive difference to&nbsp;
how hair responds to different products

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:27.480
variation in geometric shape is just not&nbsp;
something we have to think about much with skin

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.420
take wet versus dry detangling for example

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.880
the way water interacts with hair is weird

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:34.680
I'm not going to go into too much detail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.220
here because that's a whole topic&nbsp;
for like five videos but in short

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.620
it weakens the bonds gluing the inside of the hair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.140
together which is why hair&nbsp;
gets stretchy when it's wet

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.800
but it adds stickiness to the outside&nbsp;
of the hair so hairs stick together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.040
more easily and you need to use&nbsp;
more force to get the comb through

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.380
water also raises the cuticle scales

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:54.120
these are the roof tile looking cells on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.660
the surface and that means they can&nbsp;
bash against each other more and chip

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:01.800
so all of this means it's less damaging&nbsp;
to to detangle hair when it's dry

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:06.420
but that's just for straight hair&nbsp;
it is the opposite for curly hair

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:10.440
all of these effects still happen but&nbsp;
changing just the shape of the hair strand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:14.340
that is enough to change the physics&nbsp;
of how the hair interacts with a comb

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:17.640
the cuticle scales still stand&nbsp;
up more the curly hair is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:21.600
weaker on the inside and sticky on the&nbsp;
outside the hair is still more fragile

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:23.700
but there are two big differences

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:27.480
first off the hairs stick to each&nbsp;
other less because there is less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:32.220
alignment or tessellation between curly&nbsp;
hair strands compared to straight hair

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:36.840
it's like how when you cook spaghetti they&nbsp;
stick together much more than with spiral pasta

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:42.420
on top of that the weakening of the bonds inside&nbsp;
the hair actually makes the curls loosen in shape

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:44.700
a looser curl means that geometrically there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:47.220
less chance of tangling and&nbsp;
causing damage and breakage

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:50.760
so the change in the physics of how&nbsp;
that curl interacts with the comb

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:56.040
that can offset the damage from manipulating wet&nbsp;
fragile hair and overall you get less breakage

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:57.720
there's a few studies showing this

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.060
but where is the line

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:04.380
is it better to detangle say&nbsp;
wavy hair when it's wet or dry

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:08.340
what if you use a brush versus a&nbsp;
comb or a different type of brush

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:11.640
what if you detangle it from the&nbsp;
bottom up or just straight from the top

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.980
what happens if you partly dry the hair

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:15.360
those answers just

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:16.680
don't seem to exist

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.800
and we are just talking about water and hair shape

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.140
we're not even talking about other ingredients

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:23.640
how often you wash your hair

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:25.140
how much damage you have

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:26.580
the type of damage you have

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:28.260
if you bleach or straighten your hair

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:31.020
or how long your hair is or how oily it is

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:33.600
there are a lot of variables

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:39.600
and this kind of overlaps with reason&nbsp;
two hair science is just really sparse

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:42.360
we just do not know that much about hair

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:44.820
I used to assume that hair&nbsp;
was really straightforward

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:47.160
I mean hair is dead skin is alive

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:50.280
if you're doing tests on hair&nbsp;
strands it should be really easy

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:51.900
you just buy a bunch of hair

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:54.420
that is so much easier than finding volunteers

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:59.100
but because of all this diversity with&nbsp;
hair every experiment you do - you just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.800
can't necessarily generalize&nbsp;
this to other types of hair

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:05.520
on top of that there are tons of&nbsp;
other variables that make a huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.300
difference to hair like what humidity&nbsp;
the experiment started and ended up with

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:13.140
when I go places with high humidity&nbsp;
it gets a little extra body okay

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:18.420
that alone can massively change the results and&nbsp;
we all live in places with different humidities

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:22.260
and the humidity changes throughout&nbsp;
the day it changes in different rooms

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:27.660
so it isn't like if every study did experiments&nbsp;
at 20% humidity that would solve the problem

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:31.500
hair scientists don't even know the&nbsp;
structure of hair in that much detail

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:34.140
the understanding is still&nbsp;
evolving relatively quickly

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:39.540
and there just isn't that much consistency with&nbsp;
terminology even in the peer-reviewed literature

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:44.580
so for example around the late 90s some&nbsp;
hair scientists just decided half the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:48.240
protein in hair wouldn't be called keratin&nbsp;
anymore and they have good reasons for that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:52.680
but not everyone got the memo or maybe&nbsp;
some hair scientists just didn't agree

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:58.320
so you'll still read things like "hair is 80%&nbsp;
keratin by mass" even in this paper from 2017&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:02.640
and this isn't a huge deal here because it is&nbsp;
pretty clear they're using that old definition

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:05.700
but if a paper says "we concluded that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:10.020
ingredient works on keratin and hair"&nbsp;
do they mean keratin or do they mean

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:11.100
keratin

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:11.760
[muffled screaming]

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:16.740
Reason 3 hair products aren't&nbsp;
just about individual ingredients

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:19.140
the two main hair products shampoo and conditioner

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:22.320
the way they work isn't about&nbsp;
the individual ingredients the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:24.720
overall formulation is really important

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:29.760
and yeah we say that a lot about skincare&nbsp;
but it is even more the case with hair care

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:33.720
and this is central to this&nbsp;
debate about sulfates and shampoos

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:37.680
sulfates usually refers to sodium lauryl&nbsp;
sulfate and sodium laureth sulfate

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:40.440
these are two of the cleaning&nbsp;
ingredients or surfactants that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:42.840
you find in things like shampoos and cleansers

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:47.520
surfactants are special because they have a&nbsp;
head that likes water and a tail that likes oil

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:49.020
as you hopefully know from

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:49.680
Life

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:51.840
oil and water don't usually mix

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:54.600
so surfactacts can help oil break up and mingle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:57.840
with water so you can rinse it away&nbsp;
and end up with clean hair or skin

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:02.280
the tails are pretty similar in surfactants so&nbsp;
most of the time we're talking about the heads

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:04.500
sulfates have sulfate head groups

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:07.560
chemists are not imaginative&nbsp;
when it comes to naming

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:11.760
and yes if we compare sulfates on their&nbsp;
own with other surfactants on their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:17.160
they have good cleaning ability but because&nbsp;
it's hair science there is more complexity

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:21.360
these four diagrams show four different ways&nbsp;
that shampoos clean at a microscopic level

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:23.820
each of these diagrams is read from left to right

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:26.760
the pink tadpoles are the surfactants and you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:29.760
see that the surfactants are&nbsp;
coordinating to clean the hair

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:32.700
this is called a supramolecular process where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:35.760
molecules are interacting without&nbsp;
going through a chemical reaction

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:38.280
it's a bit like molecular choreography

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:41.340
now this isn't like four separate processes

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:46.200
all four of these are probably happening on&nbsp;
your hair at the same time to different extents

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:49.080
there are probably still more&nbsp;
mechanisms still to be discovered

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:53.580
how much of these depends on the formula of the&nbsp;
shampoo because the pink tadpole surfactants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:57.360
won't all be the same they'll have different&nbsp;
tendencies to interact in different ways

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:01.320
but it also depends on what sorts of&nbsp;
stuff or soils you're cleaning off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:04.320
because they are also part of this choreography

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:09.600
so for example if you have bigger blobs of oil on&nbsp;
your hair then this one is probably happening more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:14.700
because some of that oil breaks off multiple times&nbsp;
instead of all of it coming off at once like here

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:19.620
but if you have big particles that don't break&nbsp;
up easily then it might all just be figure 4

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:24.600
this one happens more at high surfactant&nbsp;
concentrations and that is another variable

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:28.440
and that's not just about the concentration&nbsp;
of the surfactants in the formula

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:32.160
at the start of the shampooing process&nbsp;
you've just put the shampoo on your hair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:36.780
it's at maximum concentration but if you&nbsp;
add more water or you start rinsing then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:40.200
the concentration will decrease and&nbsp;
you might have less of this happening

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:43.860
and this is already getting pretty&nbsp;
complicated but it's still a massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:47.520
oversimplification because hair&nbsp;
science will not give us a break

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:51.900
if you look at the ingredients of almost any&nbsp;
shampoo it'll have three or more difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:55.860
surfactants that means that these pink&nbsp;
tadpoles in the diagram aren't all the same

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:58.680
if you change the ratio of the ingredients or swap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:02.640
one of these out with something else then&nbsp;
the shampoo might work quite differently

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:06.480
plus there's a bunch of other ingredients&nbsp;
that aren't shown here like polymers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:10.620
oils and those will also interact with&nbsp;
the surfactants and affect how it cleans

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:15.300
and on top of that things like the texture of&nbsp;
the shampoo the opening of the bottle that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:18.840
going to change how much you dispense&nbsp;
and how the shampoo spreads on hair

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:21.600
so in case you didn't notice it is really complex

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:25.980
and what that means is trying to predict&nbsp;
what happens just by looking at whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:30.600
some of these pink tadpoles are sulfates or&nbsp;
not - that is just not going to tell you much

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:33.180
it just isn't as simple as "it has sulfates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:35.940
it'll clean better" or "it'll&nbsp;
strip or degrease your hair"

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:40.560
if it was it would be so much easier for&nbsp;
everyone to just find the right shampoo

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:44.460
same with the pH of shampoos I see lots&nbsp;
of people saying you have to check the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:47.880
pH and make sure it's acidic&nbsp;
and again that is just not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:51.300
very meaningful piece of information&nbsp;
when the whole thing is so complex

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:55.020
it's a bit like trying to predict how&nbsp;
a food tastes from how yellow it is

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:59.340
and even if you knew the full formula of&nbsp;
a shampoo every single ingredient what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:03.660
concentration they're in where they got&nbsp;
the ingredients it would still be pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:07.380
much impossible to predict how it's going&nbsp;
to interact with itself let alone with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:11.160
different types of soils on your hair at&nbsp;
different stages of the shampooing process

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:14.520
it takes a ridiculous amount&nbsp;
of computational power to model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:18.420
a much simpler sort of system with&nbsp;
way less moving parts than a shampoo

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:23.040
this is why cosmetic formulators spend so much&nbsp;
time just during trial and error making formulas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:27.180
and trying them out changing little things&nbsp;
about a formula can make a huge difference

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:28.440
here's one example

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:31.020
Good Housekeeping did this test with 10 shampoo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:33.300
and conditioner pairings that&nbsp;
were meant to protect colour

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:35.100
some of these had sulfates and some didn't

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:36.960
there were salon and drugstore brands

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:39.960
the set that stripped hair&nbsp;
dye the least had sulfates

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:41.160
it's this tresemme set

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:45.540
they also have this article where hair scientists&nbsp;
talk about how complex it is to formulate a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:50.460
shampoo but even with their own results as&nbsp;
proof and two scientists with PhDs explaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:54.900
to them that it's not about sulfates versus&nbsp;
no sulfates their article on color stripping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.900 --> 00:11:00.300
and shampoos still has this BS about sulfates&nbsp;
"sudding" the hair and causing pigment loss

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:01.800
these myths run deep

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:05.580
but I think a lot of us have had the&nbsp;
experience where we try a sulfate free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:09.360
shampoo and it does feel like it's&nbsp;
gentle or it doesn't clean enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:12.660
the way you frame it probably depends&nbsp;
on whether or not your hair likes that

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:15.600
and the reason for this I&nbsp;
think is a sort of circular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:18.120
reasoning that happens quite a bit with products

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:22.380
formulators know that people looking for&nbsp;
sulfate free probably want something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:25.920
cleans less they know there's all these&nbsp;
widespread myths about sulfates being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:29.400
harsh they've been going around since&nbsp;
the 90s like in this email forward

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:33.000
"SLS is used to scrub garage&nbsp;
floors and it is very strong"

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:36.120
even if the formulator didn't know&nbsp;
about all this because I don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:39.240
if they've been hiding in a hole&nbsp;
it's probably on the product brief

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:42.780
that's the description of the product&nbsp;
that the formulator works off it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:46.560
usually written by marketing people&nbsp;
based on consumer research it'll have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:49.500
the marketing claims that they plan&nbsp;
to put on the products so chances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:53.160
are the brief says "sulfate free" as&nbsp;
well as "gentle" and "not stripping"

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:56.340
so there's a good chance that if you&nbsp;
grab a random shampoo with sulfates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.340 --> 00:12:00.360
and a random shampoo without sulfates&nbsp;
the one with sulfates will clean better

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:04.620
it's not because just adding sulfates&nbsp;
automatically makes a shampoo strip more

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:07.500
we saw that it can be formulated&nbsp;
to strip colour the least

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:09.600
it's just because of intentional product design

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:13.380
the formulator formulated with&nbsp;
sulfates and to make it clean more

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:15.180
they might have also changed some of the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:17.760
ingredients to make it feel&nbsp;
like your hair is cleaner

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:21.900
but brands don't all have the same idea&nbsp;
of what counts as gentle and some brands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:25.680
choose not to follow this consumer trend&nbsp;
so there's going to be lots of exceptions

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:29.520
it's like how if you grab a shampoo in a&nbsp;
black and green bottle there's a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:33.540
good chance it'll smell minty because product&nbsp;
designers have decided that is what men like

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:36.720
this is exactly the same&nbsp;
situation with foam and cleansers

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:40.200
how well a product foams doesn't&nbsp;
tell you about how well it cleans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:42.780
what's happening on a molecular level is different

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:45.180
how well something first depends on how well it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:48.300
interacts with air how well&nbsp;
it traps air in a thin film

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:51.300
cleaning is about how well it&nbsp;
interacts with oil and dirt

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:55.740
both cleaning and foam rely on surfactants but&nbsp;
apart from that there's no real correlation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.740 --> 00:12:58.920
between which surfactants foam&nbsp;
best and which ones clean best

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:02.100
and there's so much that depends on&nbsp;
the overall formula there are lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:04.500
other ingredients that help to create a nice foam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:09.420
again both are complicated supramolecular&nbsp;
processes there are so many moving parts

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:12.720
but if something doesn't foam when&nbsp;
we use it we tend to feel like it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:16.380
not cleaning properly it's a purely&nbsp;
psychological thing and I personally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:19.740
still have this gut feeling even though&nbsp;
I know all of this chemistry behind it

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:23.100
humans did not evolve to be&nbsp;
perfectly rational in our feelings

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:25.560
so formulators make sure that shampoos and other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:28.020
cleaning products usually&nbsp;
foam really nicely for us

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:31.740
but if we don't see it then it&nbsp;
doesn't have to foam to convince us

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:36.420
one example is detergents for dishwashers they're&nbsp;
actually kind of formulated the opposite way

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:41.340
they have to clean really well with as little&nbsp;
foam as possible because foam causes pressure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.340 --> 00:13:45.240
and if that builds up then it can make&nbsp;
the dishwasher leak and flood our kitchen

00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:46.260
sploosh

00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:46.920
sploosh

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:50.280
bottom line sulfate free sulfate&nbsp;
containing doesn't really mean much

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:54.000
how well a shampoo cleans is way too&nbsp;
complicated to predict that easily

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:57.600
it's much more useful to actually look&nbsp;
at what the shampoo is trying to tell you

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:00.420
if it says clarifying then it's a shampoo designed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.420 --> 00:14:03.180
to clean your hair better doesn't&nbsp;
matter if it has sulfates or not

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:05.460
if it says colour protection it can have sulfates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:07.920
and still strip dye less&nbsp;
than a sulfate-free shampoo

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:11.040
if you stick to these rules about&nbsp;
sulfate versus sulfate free then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:13.620
you would have missed out on the&nbsp;
least colour stripping shampoo

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:15.480
it's also really useful to look at reviews&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:18.780
from people with similar hair to&nbsp;
you and try it out on your hair

